<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Sunny. I'm Happy! – Lesson # 3</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny. I'm Happy!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe simple personal experiences and habits. Interactive: Participate in simple dialogues on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe simple personal experiences with the weather (e.g., "I ride a bike on sunny days!"). Can participate in simple dialogues about the weather (e.g., "What's the weather today?" "It's rainy."). Can describe the weather and their feelings (e.g., "I am happy on sunny days.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e6e6e6;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a brief "Weather & Feelings Check-in." The teacher shows flashcards of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and corresponding feeling emojis (happy, sad, bored, excited). The teacher asks, "What's the weather today?" and "How do you feel today?" Students respond orally with single words or short phrases. E.g., "Sunny!" "Happy!"</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models full sentences, emphasizing contractions and pronunciation.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "sunny" flashcard: "It's sunny. I'm happy!" (emphasizing /s/ in sunny). Students repeat chorally.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "rainy" flashcard: "It's rainy. I'm bored." (emphasizing /r/ in rainy). Students repeat chorally.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks a student: "What's the weather today?" (Teacher pauses for student to answer). "How do you feel?" (Teacher pauses for student to answer). Teacher repeats the student's answer in a full sentence: "Yes, it's [sunny/rainy]. You're [happy/sad]."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models blending for /r-eɪn/ in "rain" and /ˈr-eɪn-i/ in "rainy" by slowly saying the sounds and then blending them, asking students to repeat.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of weather words and feelings.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a 'happy' emoji) "Am I sad?" (No!) "Am I happy?" (Yes!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a 'rainy' picture) "Is it sunny?" (No!) "Is it rainy?" (Yes!)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or on flashcards, focusing on two children discussing the weather and their preferred activities/feelings. </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                        <b>Sam:</b> "Hi, Anna! What's the weather today?"<br>
                        <b>Anna:</b> "Hi, Sam! It's sunny and warm. I'm happy!"<br>
                        <b>Sam:</b> "Me too! I like sunny days. I play outside."<br>
                        <b>Anna:</b> "What about rainy days? How do you feel?"<br>
                        <b>Sam:</b> "On rainy days, I feel bored. I stay inside. I need my umbrella!"
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Asking and answering about daily weather, expressing personal feelings, and describing simple activities related to weather using present simple. Pragmatic: Describing weather and feelings: "I'm happy." "It's raining." Asking and answering about feelings politely: "How do you feel?" "I feel happy."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Teacher reads the dialogue aloud, then asks students to answer orally.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the weather in the dialogue?" (Sunny!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does Anna feel on sunny days?" (Happy!)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does Sam need on rainy days?" (His umbrella!)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Describing weather using "It's [weather adjective]." Expressing feelings using "I'm [feeling adjective]" or "I feel [feeling adjective]." Asking and answering "How do you feel today?" Grammar: Contractions (I'm, it's), WH-questions (How do you feel?), Present Simple (I feel happy on sunny days.). Vocabulary: sunny, rainy, cloudy, happy, sad, bored, excited, umbrella.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher displays a series of incomplete sentences about weather and feelings. Students complete them orally as a class or individually.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's _____. I'm _____." (Teacher shows 'sunny' picture, students say: "It's sunny. I'm happy.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On rainy days, I feel _____." (Teacher shows 'rainy' picture and 'bored' emoji, students say: "On rainy days, I feel bored.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When it's windy, I feel _____." (Teacher shows 'windy' picture, students say: "When it's windy, I feel excited.")</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Q&A Chain Drill:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking the first student, "How do you feel today?" The student answers, "I feel [happy/sad/excited], thank you." Then, that student asks the next student, "What's the weather like today?" The next student answers, "It's [sunny/rainy/cloudy]," and then asks the next student, "How do you feel today?" The chain continues. Teacher provides sentence frames on the board for support:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do you feel today?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel _____, thank you."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the weather like today?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's _____."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Weather Chat" with Flashcards:</b> In pairs, students use a set of weather and feeling flashcards. Student A picks a weather card and asks, "What's the weather today?" Student B responds, "It's [sunny/rainy/cloudy]." Then, Student A asks, "How do you feel on [sunny/rainy/cloudy] days?" Student B answers, "I feel [happy/sad/bored] on [sunny/rainy/cloudy] days." Students then switch roles. Teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially /s/ and /r/ sounds.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Interview & Share. Students work in pairs to interview each other about their favorite weather and associated feelings/activities, then present their partner's information to the class.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each pair receives a simple "Interview Sheet" with prompts:
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What's your favorite weather?</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How do you feel on [favorite weather] days?</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What do you do on [favorite weather] days? (e.g., "I play outside," "I read a book," "I ride a bike")</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns interviewing each other, using the questions. They can draw a small picture for their partner's answer. (e.g., Student A: "What's your favorite weather, [Partner's Name]?" Student B: "My favorite weather is sunny!" Student A: "How do you feel on sunny days?" Student B: "I feel happy! I play outside.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the interviews, each student presents their partner's information to the class. E.g., "This is [Partner's Name]. [He/She] likes sunny days. [He/She]'s happy on sunny days. [He/She] plays outside."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages the use of contractions "I'm", "It's" and polite phrases like "thank you."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during the "Partner Interview & Share" activity, specifically focusing on their ability to ask and answer questions about weather and feelings, and describe simple experiences. </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in sunny, /r/ in raining) and contractions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Employs words like "sunny, rainy, happy, sad, bored, play, ride a bike."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Describes weather and feelings using simple sentences (e.g., "It's sunny. I'm happy.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Actively participates in the interview and shares partner's information.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students orally complete one of the following sentences before leaving:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can say: 'It's _____. I'm _____.'"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I learned to ask: 'How do you feel?'"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to talk about today?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new sentence you can say about the weather or how you feel?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting student speaking? (e.g., specific activity, use of visuals, sentence frames)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to improve fluency or confidence in speaking? (e.g., more practice with specific grammar points, clearer instructions for pair work, smaller groups)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking about weather and feelings. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write simple sentences and short paragraphs about weather, feelings, and activities, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced today.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>